<DocsLayout title="Haft documentation" description="Source-native FPF delivery and reliance-bearing project memory for AI-assisted engineering.">
  <h1>Haft documentation</h1>

  <p>
    Haft is a governance substrate used from an existing coding agent. It
    delivers versioned FPF source into the current question and preserves typed
    project memory when later work must rely on the result.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>This documentation covers v9.0.0, the current release.</strong>
      To check which version a host is using, inspect the loaded binary and
      host process.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The v9 product boundary</h2>

  <p>One local <code>.haft/</code> graph is available through three surfaces:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>independent host-agent skills, including <code>h-reason</code> for source-first FPF reasoning;</li>
    <li>the manual governance CLI;</li>
    <li>the <code>haft serve</code> MCP server.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    v9 does not ship a coding-agent executor. <code>haft run</code>,
    <code>haft harness</code>, Open-Sleigh, and the Elixir/OTP/BEAM runtime were
    removed. WorkCommission and RuntimeRunRecord lifecycle records remain
    runner-neutral for host-agent work or separately operated integrations.
  </p>

  <h2>Start here</h2>

  <ol>
    <li><a href="/docs/9.0/install">Install and initialize Haft</a> for the host you use.</li>
    <li>Restart the host so it loads the installed MCP server and skills.</li>
    <li>Ask the agent your current engineering question; use <code>/h-reason</code> when you want an explicit FPF-supported pass.</li>
    <li>Use <code>/h-status</code> when live project state, drift, coverage, or readiness is the question.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Authority stays explicit</h2>

  <p>
    A recommendation is not a decision, a plan is not performed work, and a
    lifecycle result is not automatically evidence truth. <code>h-decide</code>
    can route one direct, unambiguous operator choice. <code>h-commission</code>
    stays manual-only because a WorkCommission grants bounded execution
    authority.
  </p>

